                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Halloween is upon us and I have been slacking on my horror game playing lately, so I had the genius idea to take all the games with the horror tag in my Steam library that are around 3 hours or under and put them in a wheel randomizer to play through and put me in the spooky mood.\n\nI am starting to think maybe this was a bad idea. Turns out after years of buying random bundles, I might have a few true stinkers in my library. Who would have guessed it.\n\nIn The Hat Man, you in fact do not take too many Benadryl and get visited by a mysterious hat wearing stranger. Instead, you put a bunch of games in a randomizer and get stuck playing some barely functional junk.\n\nI don't like to be harsh on indie projects, but if you want to avoid harsh criticism, you at least have to make sure the game is functioning correctly. Right off the bat, to interact with anything in the game, I had to click the input multiple times for it to register. Want to open a door? Get to spamming that LMB. Want to switch items in your inventory? Spam that number key. In a game that is all about RUNNING from monsters, that is kind of a big flaw as these are actions that you want to do quickly.\n\nBut it gets worse. The game only has static save points, you cannot save on your own without reaching these. Well imagine my displeasure after an hour of gameplay, nearing what seemed to be the end of the game, I saved my game, went down a hallway and died. Load saved game. Stuck in the same hallway, die instantly. Close the game. Re-open it. Load saved game. Same deal. Yeah, hardlocking a game that doesn't have multiple save slots is a dealbreaker for me. As always, I have thick skin when it comes to jank and bad games. At the end of the day however, I gotta respect my time, and my time is not well spent on seeing a game through to the end when at any moment it can just hardlock on me with no workaround.\n\nAdd onto that some very poor voice acting with awful sound quality and no noise removal, a gameplay loop that is randomized, frequently unfair putting you in dead ends you cannot escape from safely, and in general just has you running around randomly generated, same-y hallways listening for audio queues to signify an item is nearby, stealth mechanics that quite literally do not work as the enemies will just go through walls to find you anywhere you are hiding and what you are left with is just an outright bad game with a generic premise that has been done before, to much better effect. Slenderman is literally the blueprint for this type of game, and is more functional than this one, and free.\n\nHopefully the wheel blesses me with it's next choice.",
